{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q025_classification_as_evolutionary_hypothesis","answer":"Assign it as a new superfamily within the existing fold. In SCOPe, fold membership asserts only shared secondary-structure composition, arrangement and topology; superfamily membership asserts probable common ancestry; family membership asserts close ancestry, normally with detectable sequence similarity. Because the domain shares the fold but lacks convincing evidence of homology, a new superfamily is the only placement whose evolutionary claim matches the evidence. SCOPe 2.08 applies this routinely: the TIM beta/alpha-barrel fold c.1 (sunid 51350) contains 34 separate superfamilies (c.1.1 through c.1.34) that share the barrel topology but are not asserted to be homologous to one another.

Biological claim of each choice: (1) New family in an existing superfamily claims the domain descends from the same ancestor as the other families of that superfamily, merely diverged beyond family-level sequence/structure-function criteria. Without homology evidence this overstates the relationship and would license unsafe transfer of function from superfamily relatives. (2) New superfamily in the existing fold claims shared architecture and topology only, with no claim of common ancestry; the resemblance may reflect convergence, physical constraints on packing, or ancestry too remote to detect. This is the correct, conservative hypothesis. (3) New fold claims the core topology is distinct from all existing folds; it is an architectural claim, not an ancestry claim, and is wrong unless re-examination shows the resemblance is superficial (different strand order, connectivity, or handedness in the core). 'New fold' is not the way to express 'not homologous'.

Limits and required evidence: fold similarity or a structure-alignment (FATCAT) hit alone does not establish homology, and function must not be transferred from fold-mates in other superfamilies. Demoting the domain into an existing superfamily or family requires positive homology evidence: significant profile/HMM sequence similarity, conserved catalytic or ligand-binding residues at equivalent structural positions, shared unusual structural features beyond the fold core, or shared mechanism and cofactor. The assignment is an evolutionary hypothesis that can be revised in later releases, as SCOPe has merged or split superfamilies when new intermediate sequences linked previously separate lineages.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/c.1/children?limit=100","pretrained knowledge of SCOP/SCOPe hierarchy definitions (fold = topology; superfamily = probable common ancestry; family = close ancestry)"]}]}
